"Conversations with philosophers in Vienna persuaded him that his book had left things unsettled, and in 1929 he went back to Cambridge. He needed a degree in order to teach, so he submitted the Tractatus as a doctoral thesis. Russell and G. E. Moore examined him. The story goes that he ended the examination by clapping them on the shoulder and saying, Dont worry, I know youll never understand it. For the next eighteen years he taught in his rooms, without notes, thinking aloud before a small group and often falling silent or cursing his own stupidity. He came to reject the central idea of his first book, that language has one essential function. He published nothing. During the Second World War he left his chair to work as a hospital porter in London.": "992e52c0919c",
"He gave his entire inheritance to his brother and sisters, trained as a teacher, and from 1920 taught in poor mountain villages south of Vienna. His sister said that it was like using a precision instrument to open crates. He was a demanding and impatient teacher who took the brightest children far beyond the syllabus and struck the slow ones. In 1926, after a boy he had hit collapsed, he resigned. He worked for a time as a gardener in a monastery and then spent two years designing a house for one of his sisters, with every door handle and radiator made to his own measurements.": "176cef1a2120",
"He is the only philosopher to have founded two schools that opposed each other. The Tractatus inspired the logical positivists of Vienna, who took it to show that only the statements of science have meaning and that the rest is nonsense. Wittgenstein thought they had misread him. For him the things that cannot be said were the most important, and at their meetings he sometimes turned his back and read poetry aloud. The Investigations inspired the philosophers of ordinary language at Oxford, who examined the everyday use of words with a patience he might have found tedious.": "baba5036e07d",
"He resigned his professorship in 1947 to write, and lived for a while alone in a cottage on the west coast of Ireland. Cancer was diagnosed in 1949. He spent his last weeks in the house of his doctor in Cambridge, working until two days before the end. When the doctors wife told him that his friends would arrive the next day, he said: Tell them Ive had a wonderful life. The Philosophical Investigations, on which he had worked for sixteen years, appeared two years after his death.": "373d076fabb6",
"He served for the whole war, asked for the most dangerous posts, and was decorated several times for courage. In his pack he carried notebooks, and in them he wrote the Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus. He finished it in 1918 and was a prisoner of war in Italy when he sent it to Russell. The book is seventy-five pages of numbered propositions. It argues that language pictures the facts of the world, that the limits of what can be pictured are the limits of what can be said, and that ethics, religion, and the meaning of life lie outside those limits. Its last sentence is: Whereof one cannot speak, thereof one must be silent. He believed that he had solved the problems of philosophy, and he acted on the belief by leaving the subject.": "b0dffecf8daf",
"He was trained as an engineer, and went to Manchester in 1908 to work on aircraft engines and propellers. The mathematics of propellers led him to the foundations of mathematics, and those led him to the logician Frege, who told him to go to Cambridge and study with Bertrand Russell. He arrived in 1911. Within a year Russell was saying that he had nothing left to teach him. In 1913 his father died and he inherited a fortune. When war came the following summer he enlisted in the Austrian army as a private.": "81dfde0dca2f",
"In the later work the method is to describe how words are actually used. He distrusts general theories and asks for examples. Consider the things we call games, he says: board games, card games, ball games, a child throwing a ball against a wall. If you look for what is common to all, you find nothing. You find a complicated network of similarities overlapping and criss-crossing, like the resemblances among the members of a family. His instruction is: dont think, but look! Applied to meaning, understanding, pain, and following a rule, the method yields the conclusions for which he is known. Meaning lies in use. Understanding is an ability and not an inner event. And a rule cannot be followed by one person in private.": "a595a4a1d192",
"Karl Popper pressed the same point to his face. At a meeting in Cambridge in 1946, Popper read a paper claiming that there are real philosophical problems and not merely puzzles. Witnesses disagree about what followed. Wittgenstein, who was holding the poker from the fireplace, is said to have demanded an example of a moral rule, and Popper to have replied: Not to threaten visiting lecturers with pokers. Wittgenstein left the room. His defenders say that he never denied that the problems are deep. He located the depth in us, in how hard it is to see what lies in plain view.": "ed147b70947f",
"Ludwig Wittgenstein was born in 1889 into one of the richest families in Europe. His father Karl had built the Austrian steel industry. Brahms and Mahler came to the house to play, and it held seven grand pianos. It was also an unhappy house. Karl wanted his five sons to follow him into business, and three of them killed themselves. A fourth, Paul, became a concert pianist, lost his right arm in the war, and went on performing with his left. Ludwig was the youngest. He thought of suicide for much of his youth.": "622d79087ec0",
"Readers still argue about how deep the break between the two books goes. Some see a complete reversal, from language as a mirror of the world to language as a set of activities. Others stress what did not change: the conviction that philosophy is a struggle against confusion and yields no doctrine, the interest in what can only be shown, and the moral seriousness with which he treated every sentence. He himself asked that the two books be printed together, so that the new thoughts could be seen in the right light against the old.": "1b34c834a689",
"They differ in what the dissolving is for and in how it is done. Wittgensteins treatment is intellectual. He works on a confusion until it lets go, and he promises only that the philosopher will be able to stop doing philosophy when he wants to. He had no method for the suffering he himself knew well. The Buddhas treatment includes ethics and the training of attention, since on his account the picture of a self is held in place by craving and cannot be removed by argument alone. And where Wittgenstein fell silent about what lies beyond language, the Buddhist tradition, which agrees that it cannot be said, went on to say a good deal about how to get there (Dharma and Western Philosophy D2, D3).": "590486a6eaad",
"Wittgenstein compared his method to therapy and his early book to a ladder that must be thrown away once it has been climbed. A Buddhist reader recognizes both images. The Buddha called himself a physician, declined to answer questions about the beginning of the world or the fate of a saint after death on the ground that the answers do not lead to release, and described his teaching as a raft to be left on the far shore. Both men thought that some questions should be dissolved and not answered, and that the cause of our trouble is a picture that holds us captive. For both, the chief example is the self. Wittgenstein argued that I does not name an inner object, and that the idea of a private owner of experiences comes from misreading the grammar of the word. The Buddha taught that no such owner is found in body, feeling, perception, intention, or consciousness.": "54e8d8125110",
"Wittgenstein held two philosophies, and the second is a criticism of the first. What they share is a view of what philosophy is. It is not a science and discovers no new facts. Philosophical problems arise, he thought, when language is misunderstood, when a form of words that works in one setting is carried into another and goes on holiday. We ask What is time? as we might ask What is copper? and expect the same kind of answer. The philosophers task is to trace the confusion to its source so that the problem disappears. He called the aim to show the fly the way out of the fly-bottle.": "2f63cd70b48b",
"Wittgenstein wrote that philosophy leaves everything as it is. It may describe language and may not reform it. Critics from Russell onward thought this an abdication. Russell said of the later work that it seemed to him to have abandoned serious thinking and to have invented a doctrine that would make such thinking unnecessary. If philosophical problems are only knots in language, then the questions people have asked about justice, knowledge, and the mind were mistakes, and the right response to them is therapy. Many philosophers find this incredible. Some of the questions have turned into sciences, and others have guided how people live.": "5e8f60ba7986",
